id: decisions/011-label-first-prove-before-promote
title: "Labelled action allowed, promotion waits for proof"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'proof-timing'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: proof-timing
axis_gloss: "whether the evidence must exist before the change lands, or the change ships first and acquires its proof later"
poles:
  a: act-then-prove
  b: prove-then-act
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 24
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(16) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  May a record be marked active or accepted, or be cited as another record's
  proof, before its own evidence exists — so long as it is labelled — or must
  the proof land first?

why_it_matters: >
  The written rules say durable memory waits for authority and proof and legacy
  claims stay historical until restated and proved, yet three nodes cite a
  status: proposed migration as proof.source, a pattern ledger entry reads
  accepted with proof 'pending', and eight automations run active under
  seed-status agent contracts. Once answered, a bot knows whether 'active' on
  its own contract is a lifecycle fact (it runs) or a claim it must have earned,
  and whether it may point at a proposal and call it proof.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    10 judgments sit at act-then-prove (1 authored) and 6 at prove-then-act (3
    authored). The repository asserts both directions on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave reads 'active' on an automation as a lifecycle fact (it is
    scheduled) or as a proof claim (its contract is settled); the in-flight
    status-vocabulary test treats it as lifecycle.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: A record may ship, run, be promoted or be cited as soon as its proof
      status is labelled; evidence arrives later and the label is updated when it
      does. proof.status: not_started is a valid state for an active record.
    cost: "Softens core/co-orientation-loop.yawn's final-step condition and migrations/node.yawn raw_claims_promoted_automatically: false; the pattern ledger's ten-step ladder loses its ordering."
    risk: "A proposal cited under proof.source launders into a fact for every reader who follows the pointer; this already happened three times on one migration."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None can enforce lateness; the record would be removing the ordering claim from records/yawn.bot-pattern-ledger.yawn entry_shape and the 'before' from core-loop.yawn feedback_gate."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0044"
      - "J0325"
      - "J0406"
      - "J0636"
      - "J0647"
      - "J0683"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Nothing is promoted to active, accepted or v1, cited under proof.source,
      published, or written to durable memory until its proof condition is met.
      Working papers wait for review; automations wait for their governing agent
      contract to leave seed status.
    cost: "Unpublishes or relabels references/interception.yawn (proof-attached public working paper), demotes eight active automations, and contradicts the authored choice in J0683 to publish before peer review with a label."
    risk: "The repo stops moving; fifteen of seventeen examples already sit at not_started and would never be allowed to be used."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-proof-refs.mjs: any active record whose proof.status is not passed, proof-attached or outcome-attached fails; today roughly 90 records fail."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0752"
      - "J0772"
      - "J0894"
      - "J0913"
      - "J1033"
      - "J1207"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Provisional action is allowed when labelled (publish a paper marked
      not-peer-reviewed, run an automation marked active-seed, ship an example with
      proof not_started). Three things wait for proof: promotion of status to
      accepted, v1 or official; citation under proof.source (a status: proposed or
      draft record may be cited under proposal_refs: or source_refs:, never under
      proof.source); and durable memory update. Written once in
      core/proof-and-boundary.yawn transition_boundary.
    cost: "Re-key three proof.source lines (database/node.yawn, feedback/node.yawn, .github/node.yawn), return one ledger entry to human_review or attach its proof, one new sentence in core/proof-and-boundary.yawn."
    risk: "The label becomes the excuse; active-seed does forever what active may not."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend scripts/validate-proof-refs.mjs (in-flight): a path under proof.source whose target's top-level status is proposed or draft is an error; extend tests/status-vocabulary.test.mjs or a ledger test so an entry with state: accepted and proof_after_merge empty or 'pending' fails; tests/inquiry-selection-receipt-v0.1.test.mjs already asserts options fail before a receipt can be composed, which is this rule for receipts."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0636"
      - "J0683"
      - "J0752"
      - "J0894"
      - "J0913"
      - "J1033"
      - "J1207"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    The three authored prove-then-act judgments are all about promotion and
    durable memory; the one authored act-then-prove judgment is about labelled
    publication. They do not collide, and the two proof-attached judgments
    (publish the labelled working paper; throw before compose) each sit on the
    side C assigns them. The repo's own promotion practice is already B for canon
    (only records with a reducer, schema or test reached v1, outcome-attached), so
    C names what the tree does. The violations that remain are three proof.source
    lines and one ledger state, both cheap and both checkable by the in-flight
    proof-refs validator.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "core/proof-and-boundary.yawn transition_boundary names the three things that wait for proof."
    - "No proof.source in the tree points at a record whose status is proposed or draft, and validate-proof-refs rejects one that does."
    - "records/yawn.bot-pattern-ledger.yawn entry 2026-07-01 carries a real proof_after_merge ref or its state returns to human_review."
    - "npm test passes with those checks present."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A record reaches status accepted, official or v1 with proof.status
    not_started, or a new node cites a proposed migration under proof.source, and
    validate passes.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0044"
  - "J0325"
  - "J0406"
  - "J0636"
  - "J0647"
  - "J0683"
  - "J0752"
  - "J0772"
  - "J0894"
  - "J0913"
  - "J1033"
  - "J1207"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
